#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550069} :activity/id #uuid "5c4d6eb4-7017-503a-8745-027902f85b4a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048396 :topic/id #uuid "9bd96abb-b01a-56d0-979c-1ad6fd88bbd9" :topic/math-academy-id 1845} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550029 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549933 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39a34a4f-1593-5d63-b334-f02644c56702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+10\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549434} {:answer/id #uuid "55944f99-38ba-5956-a71f-f26ded5dafc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+8\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549490} {:answer/id #uuid "69933427-3584-5ac6-98d7-1ff965b647fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549517} {:answer/id #uuid "6f953edf-c154-5b56-92ae-08928c8b9cd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+8\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549527} {:answer/id #uuid "c5d1b184-251e-5e5e-89ab-a08574249834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+8\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+3q=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549685}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549490} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc10534a-67ee-5ca6-8585-6c869ec06248" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "11a66deb-d8c3-5e00-97b4-215e597d7249" :question/math-academy-id "q-345116" :question/problem "![](images/22/22eff0b687ad9b417114aaf033cfe52186c884df00177c74ec7e85212b746c37.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $16\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=16\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=16I=16\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.25}=4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=2\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n8=16\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+4q+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+8\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=4.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "12b95a92-17f6-5cae-a853-eda0fa52f6c6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550116}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550053 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549931 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "094355c6-339b-5600-bc50-c00a85863511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{20}{3}q=\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549327} {:answer/id #uuid "099d6715-1415-56a0-8882-278bab083639" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{20}{3}q=\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549328} {:answer/id #uuid "1739b26a-b723-512d-ba44-6579185b3864" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{20}{3}q=\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549360} {:answer/id #uuid "297d30f9-49e1-5a3f-a311-17f301f4f32e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{20}{3}q=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549406} {:answer/id #uuid "617a89cb-425d-5ab9-996b-e93f3978f5e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{3}{20}q=\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549508}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549328} :answer-field/id #uuid "7715413d-38fa-550a-b8f7-7de3c69db2ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0f78e83e-c080-5c56-8a40-9938a1632481" :question/math-academy-id "q-345102" :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $45\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.01\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=15\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "We have the following circuit diagram.\n\n![](images/34/34ed425a41460e1fca35a028c2bd7add953d0b354b28a4d4f2ab02bab67a78a8.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=45\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=45I=45\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.01}=100q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=15\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=15\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=15\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=15\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n9=45\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q+15\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{20}{3}q=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4b09538c-0eba-54f9-a003-2884f9fef33b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550029}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550069 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120341 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb542746-c55a-5c6f-8a8c-4f5604d16c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316176}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316176} :answer-field/id #uuid "c06a3a52-28bd-59ee-a7cc-c175a5c8973c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f22963ff-b55b-5a8b-88e3-83527086fce7" :question/math-academy-id "q-345014" :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $34\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $\\frac{1}{24}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=12\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." :question/worked-solution "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](images/d8/d8303b679d4e00bac0f59f51941a2842473e050033f87f56f099b853431ac5aa.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops. In an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=34\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=34I=34\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{(1/24)}=24q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=12\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=12\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=12\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=12\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+34\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+24q=9.\n$$\n\nThe *steady-state charge* is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n12(0)+34(0)+24q=9,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n24q=9\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{9}{24}=\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{3}{8}$ coulombs."} :step/id #uuid "773b4d4f-44c0-5a20-8834-084981dec492" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550053}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550093 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550001 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "890c7a0f-eb41-5cd2-9509-2acfa23ba08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549574} {:answer/id #uuid "8d15a229-95d2-597d-97a4-d3166d24f5d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549588} {:answer/id #uuid "be724bb9-4472-5843-8dcd-012063abd5ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549676} {:answer/id #uuid "d0257ea1-3e37-5482-a000-28ab4a7a6c7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549702} {:answer/id #uuid "e229bcbf-bc4f-5132-9ebe-150dc6395882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549732}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549702} :answer-field/id #uuid "9754567d-3b2e-5814-b10e-242c23c361c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d2226eab-aaf4-5587-9e42-6e7839242b16" :question/math-academy-id "q-345131" :question/problem "![](images/34/34df0d1bec2fbee41f1d2b81711e83ed3def50d1a439ad2543e7d55743638741.png)\n\nConsider the RCL circuit shown above. Given that the charge $q(t)$ of the capacitor at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nq(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-t}+\\frac{P}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{Q}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $B,$ $P,$ and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P+Q.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $6\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=6\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=6I=6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.25}=4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=2\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+4q+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+2{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+3(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\ +2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+(-6\\alpha -2\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +6\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ -6\\alpha -2\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{10},$ $\\beta =-\\frac{1}{10}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-t}-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=-3,$ $Q=-1,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=-3+(-1)=-4.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b5afdce3-a54a-570f-a847-666b5447e1e9"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550116 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318976 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69552e60-6c64-5b69-ae78-1dbd1d881d62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317928}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317928} :answer-field/id #uuid "274bab22-c3a0-54d5-8b68-b9bd43f73177" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c7bd95ee-cca5-57b9-9825-7daba3c299ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-345122" :question/problem "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=24.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." :question/worked-solution "The *steady-state charge* is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0,\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the steady-state charge, we substitute these values into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=24\n$$\n\nSubstituting the steady-state conditions gives\n\n$$\n0+4(0)+8q=24,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n8q=24\\,⟹\\,q=3.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged and the current $I=\\frac{dq}{dt}$ has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $3$ coulombs."} :step/id #uuid "f7092bd8-cf9e-5db0-846f-127249b99cc8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550093}}] :activity/title "Modeling Capacitor Charge in RCL Circuits With Second-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549305}]}
